@startuml
' REVIEWED_MAIN_2026_05_13: aligned to current merged code; large-investment retained pending simplification merge.
title WYSIWYS for Corda Protocol V1 — Tier 2 BAU Operations\n(Corrected: What Each Layer Actually Verifies)
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
skinparam responseMessageBelowArrow true
skinparam wrapWidth 460
autonumber

box "Environment A (Assumed Untrusted)" #LightPink
actor "Protocol Manager\n(Initiator)" as Manager
participant "Web UI / dApp\n(Untrusted)" as UI
end box

box "Communication" #White
participant "Slack/PagerDuty\n(OOB Channel)" as OOB
end box

box "Environment B (More Secure, non Corda CI/CD)" #LightYellow
actor "Tier 2 Signer\n(Approver)" as Approver
participant "OOB Multisig Client\n(e.g., Squads App)" as App
end box

box "Environment C (Zero-Trust HW)" #LightGreen
participant "Advanced HW\n(Keystone/GridPlus)" as HW
end box

box "Infrastructure" #LightBlue
participant "Private RPC" as RPC
participant "Blowfish API\n(Simulator)" as Sim
database "Solana Mainnet\n(Squads V4)" as Squads
end box

== Phase 1: On-Chain Proposal ==
note over Manager, Approver
**Note:** In most cases the Initiator and Approver are the **same person** (single-signer flow).  In multisig flows (shown here), they are different roles — the separation enables independent verification.
end note

Manager -> UI: Draft BAU Action\n(e.g., Transfer 50k USDC to Kamino)
UI -> Squads: `vault_transaction_create`\n& `vault_transaction_add_instruction`\n(inner IX stored in Proposal PDA)
Squads --> UI: Returns Proposal Index #88

note over UI #MistyRose
**RISK: Compromised UI**
If the Web UI is malicious, the inner instructions
written to the Proposal PDA may differ from what
the Manager intended. The on-chain payload
is the source of truth, not the UI display.
end note

== Phase 2: Out-of-Band Hand-off ==
Manager -> OOB: "Tier 2: Please approve pending proposal.\nIntent: Transfer 50k USDC to Kamino."
OOB -> Approver: Receives notification (serves only as an alert)

note over OOB #MistyRose
**RISK: Social Engineering**
OOB message is human-authored free text.
A compromised Manager or spoofed Slack
account could state a benign intent for
a malicious on-chain proposal.
end note

== Phase 3: Intent Verification via Polling & Simulation ==
note over App #PaleGreen
**MITIGATION 1: HARDCODED CONFIG POLLING**
The OOB Multisig Client ignores user input for addresses
and auto-polls the Solana RPC for the **hardcoded multisig PDA**,
entirely defeating address poisoning and UI spoofing.

**MITIGATION 2: PRIMARY INTENT VERIFICATION**
The Client operates **out-of-band** from the untrusted Web UI.
It decodes/simulates the fetched **inner instructions** from the
proposal to display a true, human-readable state change.
end note

App -> RPC: Poll active Proposal PDAs\nfor hardcoded Multisig address
RPC --> App: Returns active proposals & VaultTransaction inner IX bytes
App -> Sim: Simulate inner IX execution for active proposals
Sim --> App: Returns decoded state changes:\n"-50,000 USDC from Vault ATA"\n"+50,000 USDC to Kamino ATA"
App -> Approver: Display pending Proposal #88\n& validated intent

note over App, Sim #MistyRose
**RISK: Compromised OOB Multisig Client / Simulator**
If the OOB multisig client or simulator is compromised,
it could display a fake decode matching the OOB intent
while the actual Proposal PDA contains different IX.
**Mitigation:** Use multiple independent simulators
(Blowfish, Helius, self-hosted) on different devices.
**Mitigation:** Manually inspect Proposal PDA accounts
via a separate block explorer (Solscan, SolanaFM).
end note

== Phase 4: Hardware Wallet — Program & Account Verification ==
note over App, HW
**IMPORTANT: What the HW wallet actually signs**
The HW wallet signs an `ApproveTransaction` instruction
to the Squads program — NOT the inner transfer IX.
The inner IX (Transfer 50k USDC) lives in the Proposal PDA
and is **not part of the payload** sent to the HW wallet.
end note

App -> HW: Pass `proposal_approve` TX\nvia QR Code / WebUSB

note right of HW
**HW SCREEN (with Squads firmware support):**
Program: Squads V4 (SQDS...4nkn)
Action:  proposal_approve
Multisig: Bk4R...mN21
Member:  7xF3...9aB2 (signer)

**HW SCREEN (without Squads firmware support):**
Program: SMPLec...4nkn
Data:    0x03 58 00 00 ...
Acct[0]: 7xF3...9aB2 (signer, writable)
⚠️ Unrecognized instruction
end note

note over HW #MistyRose
**RISK: No Inner Intent on HW Screen**
The hardware wallet **NEVER** displays
"Transfer 50,000 USDC to Kamino".
It can only show the Squads wrapper instruction.
The HW provides:
✓ Correct program ID (not a phishing contract)
✓ Correct multisig PDA address
✓ Signer key identity
✓ Private key protection (secure element)
✗ Inner instruction intent (USDC transfer details)
end note

HW -> Approver: HW Screen displays:\n**Squads: proposal_approve**\nMultisig: Bk4R...mN21\nMember: 7xF3...9aB2

== Phase 5: Final Intent Check (Based on OOB Multisig Client Simulation) ==
note over Approver
**FINAL INTENT CHECK**
Approver makes the sign/reject decision based primarily
on the **OOB Multisig Client simulation output** (Phase 3):

**"Does the simulated state change match the stated intent?"**
  OOB Intent: "Transfer 50k USDC to Kamino"
  Simulation: "-50,000 USDC / +50,000 to Kamino ATA" → ✓ Match

The HW screen (Phase 4) adds a secondary check:
  Correct Squads program ID + correct multisig address → ✓

The OOB Multisig Client simulation is the **primary source of truth**
for the approver's intent verification.
end note

note over Approver #MistyRose
**RESIDUAL RISK**
If both the OOB Multisig Client (Phase 3) AND the OOB
channel (Phase 2) are compromised simultaneously,
the HW wallet alone cannot detect a malicious inner IX
because it never sees the inner instruction bytes.
This is the fundamental limitation of multisig
clear signing on current hardware wallets.
end note

Approver -> HW: Confirms & physically presses Sign
HW --> App: Returns Cryptographic Signature\n(signs only the `proposal_approve` TX)

== Phase 6: Broadcast & Timelock ==
App -> RPC: Broadcast signed `proposal_approve` TX
RPC -> Squads: Execute `ApproveTransaction`
Squads --> App: State: Approver added

note over Squads: **Tier 2 Logic:**\nIf 2/5 Threshold met:\nStatus = 'Approved'\n**24h Timelock SLA begins.**

note over Squads #PaleGreen
**MITIGATION: Timelock as Last Defence**
Even if all prior checks were fooled, the **24h timelock**
provides a final window to detect and **veto** the action.
Requires active monitoring of approved proposals
against expected actions (watchtower / alerting).
This is an **on-chain guarantee** — cannot be bypassed
without compromising the multisig threshold itself.
end note

App -> Approver: "Success. 24h Timelock Active."

@enduml
